'kerala-first-timers' => array(
    'title' => 'Kerala for first-timers: Munnar, Thekkady and a houseboat',
    'cat' => 'India', 'group' => 'kerala',
    'desc' => 'A first Kerala trip: the classic Cochin, Munnar, Thekkady and Alleppey route, the drive times, how many nights to give each stop and when to go.',
    'lead' => 'Kerala\'s classic route moves from tea hills to spice forests to backwaters in under a week. Here is how the pieces fit together.',
    'sections' => array(
        array('The classic route', '<p>Most first trips start in Cochin (Kochi) and follow this loop:</p><ol><li><strong>Cochin:</strong> Fort Kochi, the Chinese fishing nets and the old Jewish quarter.</li><li><strong>Munnar:</strong> tea estates, Mattupetty dam and Eravikulam National Park.</li><li><strong>Thekkady:</strong> Periyar lake and spice plantations.</li><li><strong>Alleppey:</strong> an overnight houseboat on the backwaters.</li></ol><p>Our Munnar Thekkady Alleppey tour covers this in 4 nights / 5 days.</p>'),
        array('How long to stay in each place', '<p>Give Munnar two nights: the first day is mostly the drive up from Cochin. Thekkady needs one night, and the houseboat is one night. With two more nights you can add Kovalam\'s beaches and a day trip to Kanyakumari.</p>'),
        array('Drive times', '<p>The roads are winding hill roads, so distances look short but take time. Plan on around 4 hours from Cochin to Munnar, similar from Munnar to Thekkady, and 3–4 hours from Thekkady down to Alleppey.</p>'),
        array('When to go', '<p>September to March is the most comfortable, with cool hills and pleasant backwaters. April and May are hot on the coast. The south-west monsoon (roughly June to August) brings heavy rain; the hills are lush and prices lower, but some activities are limited.</p>'),
        array('What to pack', '<p>Light cottons, a warm layer for Munnar evenings, rain protection in the monsoon, and modest clothes for temples. Comfortable shoes help in the tea estates.</p>'),
        array('A 5-day plan', '<ol><li>Arrive in Cochin and drive to Munnar; evening at leisure.</li><li>Munnar sightseeing: tea estates and the tea museum, Mattupetty dam, Echo Point and Eravikulam National Park (home of the Nilgiri tahr; the park closes for part of the year).</li><li>Munnar to Thekkady: spice plantations and the Periyar lake area.</li><li>Thekkady to Alleppey: board your houseboat around midday and cruise the backwaters.</li><li>Check out after breakfast and transfer to Cochin for your journey home.</li></ol><p>This is our 4-night Munnar Thekkady Alleppey tour.</p>'),
        array('Adding the coast', '<p>With two more nights, continue from the houseboat to Kovalam for its crescent beaches and lighthouse, and take a day trip to Kanyakumari, India\'s southern tip, where the sunrise and sunset over the sea draw crowds. Fly out of Trivandrum (Thiruvananthapuram) instead of Cochin. Our 6-night tour follows this route.</p>'),
        array('Food to try', '<p>Kerala meals are a highlight: appam with stew, puttu and kadala curry for breakfast, Kerala-style fish curry and karimeen (pearl spot) on the backwaters, and a traditional sadya served on a banana leaf. Vegetarian food is widely available and delicious.</p>'),
        array('Who it suits', '<p>Kerala suits almost everyone: honeymooners (misty hills and a private houseboat), families (easy sightseeing, wildlife and beaches) and senior citizens (gentle pace, comfortable stays). The main challenge is winding hill roads, so tell us if anyone is prone to motion sickness and we can plan shorter driving days.</p>'),
    ),
    'faqs' => array(
        array('Is 5 days enough for Kerala?', '<p>Yes for Munnar, Thekkady and a houseboat. Allow 6–7 days to add Kovalam and Kanyakumari.</p>'),        array('Which is better, Alleppey or Kumarakom?', '<p>Both are on the same backwaters. Alleppey has the most houseboats and the classic canal cruises; Kumarakom, on Vembanad lake, is quieter with lakeside resorts. For a first trip, an Alleppey houseboat is the usual choice.</p>'),

    ),
),

'alleppey-houseboat-guide' => array(
    'title' => 'An Alleppey houseboat night: what to expect',
    'cat' => 'India', 'group' => 'kerala',
    'desc' => 'What an Alleppey (Alappuzha) houseboat stay includes: check-in and check-out times, meals, rooms, day cruise versus overnight, and tips for families.',
    'lead' => 'A night on a Kerala houseboat is the highlight of most trips. Knowing how the day runs helps you enjoy it.',
    'sections' => array(
        array('How the day runs', '<p>Houseboats usually board around midday. You cruise the backwaters through the afternoon, past paddy fields, villages and narrow canals, then moor for the night by early evening (boats stop cruising after dark). Check-out is usually the next morning after breakfast.</p>'),
        array('Meals on board', '<p>An overnight houseboat usually includes lunch, evening tea, dinner and breakfast, cooked on board in Kerala style: fish curry, appam, vegetable dishes and rice. Tell us about vegetarian, Jain or children\'s food needs in advance so the crew can prepare.</p>'),
        array('Rooms and categories', '<p>Houseboats range from one to several bedrooms. Categories differ mainly in air-conditioning hours, room size and the upper-deck sitting area. For families or groups, a private multi-room boat is more comfortable than sharing.</p>'),
        array('Day cruise or overnight?', '<p>A day cruise gives you a few hours on the water. The overnight stay is quieter and more relaxing, and you see the backwaters in the early morning. We recommend the overnight if your schedule allows.</p>'),
        array('Tips', '<ul><li>Carry mosquito repellent for the evening.</li><li>Keep a small bag for the night; big suitcases take up cabin space.</li><li>The air-conditioning may run only at night on some categories.</li></ul>'),
    ),
    'faqs' => array(),
),

'mysore-ooty-coorg-which' => array(
    'title' => 'Mysore with Ooty, Coorg or Wayanad: which to choose',
    'cat' => 'India', 'group' => 'south-india',
